        "text_Q8kwt7": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "margin_top_mobile": "mt-0",
            "margin_bottom_mobile": "mb-0",
            "padding_top_mobile": "pt-0",
            "padding_bottom_mobile": "pb-0",
            "margin_top_desktop": "desktop:mt-0",
            "margin_bottom_desktop": "desktop:mb-0",
            "padding_top_desktop": "desktop:pt-0",
            "padding_bottom_desktop": "desktop:pb-0",
            "max_width_desktop": "135ch",
            "richtext": "<p>Smaller utility basements, laundry nooks, and storage rooms under 250 sq ft do not need a large-room powerhouse. The<a href=\"https://www.blueair.com/products/blue-pure-511i-max\"> <span style=\"text-decoration:underline\">Blue Pure 511i Max</span></a> covers 237 sq ft in 12.5 minutes at a CADR of Pollen 174 cfm, Dust 159 cfm, Smoke 153 cfm. At just 4.2 lbs and only 19 dB on its lowest setting, the unit fits quietly in a corner without taking up floor space. Smart features include auto mode, a PM2.5 sensor, app control, and night mode.</p><h3><strong>What to Look for in a Basement Air Purifier</strong></h3><p><strong>HEPA filtration plus activated carbon.</strong> HEPA catches mold spores, dust, and fine particulates. Activated carbon handles VOCs, gases, and odors. A purifier with only one of the two will fall short in a basement environment where both particle and gas-phase pollution are present.</p><p><strong>CADR matched to the space.</strong> An undersized purifier running at full speed all day will never fully clean the air. Match the rated coverage area to the actual square footage, and aim for a unit that can cycle the full room in 12 to 15 minutes.</p><p><strong>A built-in air quality sensor.</strong> Basements fluctuate. A PM2.5 sensor that triggers automatic fan speed adjustment means the purifier responds to actual conditions, not just a manual setting. All of the<a href=\"https://www.blueair.com/collections/air-purifiers\"> <span style=\"text-decoration:underline\">Blueair purifiers</span></a> on this list include auto mode for exactly that reason.</p><p><strong>Continuous operation.</strong> Running the purifier on a consistent schedule rather than only when the basement is occupied keeps mold spores, dust, and VOCs from building up between visits.</p><p><strong>A note on humidity:</strong> An air purifier removes particles and odors but will not reduce moisture levels. For chronically damp basements, pairing a HEPA air purifier with a dedicated dehumidifier gives the best results. The EPA recommends keeping indoor humidity<a href=\"https://www.epa.gov/mold/brief-guide-mold-moisture-and-your-home\"> <span style=\"text-decoration:underline\">below 60%, ideally between 30% and 50%</span></a>, to prevent mold growth.</p><h3><strong>Quick Size Guide</strong></h3><p><strong>Basement Size</strong></p><p><strong>Recommended Pick</strong></p><p><strong>Under 250 sq ft</strong></p><p>Blue Pure 511i Max</p><p><strong>250 to 400 sq ft</strong></p><p>Blue Pure 311i Max</p><p><strong>400 to 475 sq ft</strong></p><p>Blue Pure 311i+ Max</p><p><strong>475 to 700 sq ft</strong></p><p>Blue Pure 211i Max</p><p><strong>Large finished space</strong></p><p>Blue Signature</p><p>The right<a href=\"https://www.blueair.com/blogs/news/air-purifiers-basement\"> <span style=\"text-decoration:underline\">air purifier for a basement</span></a> depends most on square footage and whether humidity or odors are the primary concern. Start with the size of the space, then factor in the extras. For help matching a unit to a specific room, the<a href=\"https://www.blueair.com/pages/guided-selling\"> <span style=\"text-decoration:underline\">Blueair guided selling tool</span></a> walks through the key questions in about a minute.</p><h3><strong>FAQs</strong></h3><h4><strong>Do air purifiers help with basement mold? </strong></h4><p>Air purifiers capture mold spores in the air but do not address the source of mold growth. Always fix moisture problems first, then use a HEPA air purifier to manage airborne spores.</p><h4><strong>What CADR do I need for a basement? </strong></h4><p>For a 500-600 sq ft finished basement, look for a CADR of at least 250-300 CFM to achieve 4-5 air changes per hour for effective air cleaning.</p><h4><strong>Can an air purifier remove musty basement smells? </strong></h4><p>A HEPA air purifier with an activated carbon filter layer can significantly reduce musty odors. Carbon filters absorb the VOCs and gases that create the smell.</p><h4><strong>Should I run my basement air purifier all the time? </strong></h4><p>Yes, running it continuously on auto mode gives the best results. Basements often have stagnant air, so consistent cycling is more effective than periodic use.</p><h4><strong>Is a dehumidifier or an air purifier better for a basement? </strong></h4><p>Use both if possible. A dehumidifier controls moisture to prevent mold growth. An air purifier captures airborne particles like mold spores, dust, and allergens already in the air.</p><h4><strong>What size air purifier do I need for a 1,000 sq ft basement? </strong></h4><p>For a 1,000 sq ft space, consider two air purifiers placed strategically, or one high-capacity unit. The Blueair Blue Pure 211i Max covers up to 635 sq ft at 4.8 ACH.</p>",
            "richtext_alignment": "text-left",
            "richtext_style": "p2"
          }
        }
